g('bill-of-lading-explained', 'docs', 'Bill of lading|what it is and why it matters',
  'What a bill of lading is, its three jobs, the main types, and what to check on it before you accept or pay.',
  'The bill of lading (BL) is the most important document in ocean shipping. It does three jobs at once.',
  [('Three jobs', ['Receipt: the carrier confirms it has taken the cargo, in the stated condition and quantity.', 'Contract of carriage: it records the terms under which the carrier moves the cargo.', 'Document of title: whoever holds the original can claim the cargo, in the case of an order bill.']),
   ('Main types', [('table', ['Type', 'What it means'], [['Original (order) BL', 'Needed to collect the cargo. Can be endorsed to transfer ownership.'], ['Straight BL', 'Names the consignee. Not freely transferable.'], ['Telex release or surrendered BL', 'Originals are given up at the origin, and the cargo is released without them.'], ['Sea waybill', 'A receipt and contract only. No original needed.']])]),
   ('What to check', ['Names and addresses of shipper, consignee and notify party.', 'Description, weight, quantity and marks match the invoice and packing list.', 'Container and seal numbers, vessel, voyage and ports.', 'Freight prepaid or collect, as agreed.', 'No remarks about damage ("clean" bill) unless true.'])],
  [('Who issues the BL?', 'The shipping line (master BL) or the forwarder or NVOCC (house BL).'),
   ('What is a clean BL?', 'One with no remarks about damaged or defective cargo or packing. Banks usually need a clean BL.'),
   ('Can I change the BL after issue?', 'Yes, with the carrier consent and usually a fee. Amendments after sailing cost more.')],
  ('bl-awb-draft', 'Open the BL and AWB draft tool'))
g('house-bill-of-lading-vs-master-bill-of-lading', 'docs', 'House BL vs master BL|who issues what',
  'The difference between a house bill of lading and a master bill of lading, who holds each and why LCL cargo always has both.',
  'When a forwarder or NVOCC books space, two bills of lading exist for the same cargo. They are issued by different parties.',
  [('Master bill of lading', 'Issued by the shipping line to the forwarder or NVOCC as the shipper. It covers the whole container or consolidation.'),
   ('House bill of lading', 'Issued by the forwarder or NVOCC to the real shipper. It names the real consignee and covers that shipper cargo only.'),
   ('Why it matters', ['The consignee collects the cargo from the destination agent using the house BL.', 'Freight and charges are billed on the house BL terms.', 'In LCL, one master BL covers many house BLs.']),
   ('Watch for', 'A mismatch between the two, for example a different consignee or description, can hold the cargo at the destination.')],
  [('Do I need both?', 'As a shipper you hold the house BL. The master BL stays between the forwarder and the line.'),
   ('Is a house BL legally valid?', 'Yes, when issued by a forwarder acting as a carrier or NVOCC.'),
   ('Which one shows on the customs manifest?', 'In India, the house BL data is filed in the manifest by the NVOCC or consolidator for each consignee.')],
  ('bl-awb-draft', 'Open the BL and AWB draft tool'))
g('telex-release-and-surrendered-bill-of-lading', 'docs', 'Telex release and surrendered BL|release cargo without originals',
  'What a telex release or surrendered bill of lading is, how it speeds up release at destination, and the risks to know.',
  'A telex release lets the consignee collect the cargo without the paper originals. It is common on short routes, where couriers would be slower than the ship.',
  [('How it works', 'The shipper hands the full set of original bills of lading back to the carrier or agent at the origin. The carrier then sends a message, the telex release, to the destination office to release the cargo to the named consignee.'),
   ('When to use it', 'When the buyer is trusted or has already paid, and the cargo reaches the port before the documents could.'),
   ('Risks', ['Once the originals are surrendered, you lose control of the cargo. Do not release before payment if you rely on the BL as security.', 'Check that the consignee named on the BL is the one who will collect.']),
   ('Related options', 'A sea waybill works the same way from the start. An electronic bill of lading is the paperless form of an original.')],
  [('Is a telex release safe?', 'It is safe when payment is secure. If you are not paid, you have given up the cargo.'),
   ('Does it cost extra?', 'Many lines charge a small release fee.'),
   ('Can I get a telex release for a switched BL?', 'Yes, but the switch must be done first.')],
  ('bl-awb-draft', 'Open the BL and AWB draft tool'))
g('sea-waybill-vs-bill-of-lading', 'docs', 'Sea waybill vs bill of lading|which to choose',
  'The difference between a sea waybill and a bill of lading, when each is used and what it means for payment security.',
  'A sea waybill moves the cargo without a document of title. It is quick, but it gives you less control.',
  [('Bill of lading', 'An original BL controls the cargo. The buyer cannot collect it without the original. Good for payment on documents, or when the cargo may be sold while at sea.'),
   ('Sea waybill', 'Names the consignee, and the carrier releases the cargo to that named party on proof of identity. There is no original to carry.'),
   ('Choosing', [('table', ['Situation', 'Better choice'], [['New buyer, payment not secure', 'Original bill of lading'], ['Trusted buyer, advance paid', 'Sea waybill or telex release'], ['Letter of credit that asks for a BL', 'Original bill of lading'], ['Intra company shipment', 'Sea waybill']])])],
  [('Can a bank accept a sea waybill?', 'Only if the letter of credit allows it.'),
   ('Is a sea waybill cheaper?', 'It saves courier costs and release fees.'),
   ('Can the consignee be changed?', 'Only by the shipper instruction to the carrier, with a fee.')],
  ('bl-awb-draft', 'Open the BL and AWB draft tool'))
g('airway-bill-mawb-hawb-explained', 'docs', 'Airway bill (AWB)|master and house explained',
  'What an air waybill is, the difference between the master AWB and house AWB, the 11 digit number and what to check on it.',
  'The air waybill (AWB) is the contract and receipt for air cargo. Unlike a bill of lading, it is not a document of title.',
  [('The number', 'An AWB number has 11 digits: a three digit airline prefix, a seven digit serial and a check digit. The prefix tells you which airline carries it. You use the number to track the cargo.'),
   ('Master and house', 'The airline issues the master AWB (MAWB) to the forwarder. The forwarder issues a house AWB (HAWB) to the shipper. One MAWB can cover many HAWBs in a consolidation.'),
   ('What to check', ['Shipper, consignee and notify details.', 'Airports, flight and date.', 'Pieces, gross weight, chargeable weight and rate.', 'Description of goods and the handling or special codes.', 'Prepaid or collect, as agreed.']),
   ('Not a title document', 'The consignee gets the goods on identity, not by holding a paper original. For payment security, use a letter of credit or consign the goods to the bank.')],
  [('Can I track by AWB number?', 'Yes, on the airline website or in our tracking tool.'),
   ('What is an e-AWB?', 'An electronic AWB that removes the paper. Most large airlines support it.'),
   ('Who fills in the AWB?', 'The shipper provides the data. The forwarder or airline prints it.')],
  ('awb-label-generator', 'Open the AWB label generator'))
g('commercial-invoice-requirements', 'docs', 'Commercial invoice|what it must contain',
  'What a commercial invoice must show for customs: parties, description, HS code, value, Incoterm, origin and declarations.',
  'The commercial invoice is the basis of customs value. A weak one is the first reason for queries and delays.',
  [('Must have', ['Seller and buyer names and addresses, and the importer ID such as IEC or EORI.', 'Invoice number and date.', 'Full description of each item, not just a model number.', 'Quantity, unit price and total, with the currency.', 'Incoterm and named place, and the payment terms.']),
   ('Helpful additions', ['HS code, country of origin and weight for each item.', 'Freight and insurance shown separately.', 'Bank details and the shipment details (vessel or flight).', 'A signed declaration that the information is true.']),
   ('Common errors', ['Vague descriptions such as "spare parts" or "samples".', 'An invoice value that is different from the bank payment.', 'Missing currency or a total that does not add up.']),
   ('Free of charge goods', 'Even if the goods are free, issue an invoice that states a value for customs purposes and marks it "no commercial value" or "free of charge", as rules require.')],
  [('Is a proforma invoice acceptable?', 'No. Customs asks for the final commercial invoice.'),
   ('Does the invoice need a signature?', 'Many customs offices and banks expect one. It is a good practice.'),
   ('In which language?', 'English is safe for India. Some countries need a local language translation.')],
  ('document-generator', 'Open the document generator'))
g('packing-list-guide', 'docs', 'Packing list|what to include and why',
  'What a packing list is, what to include, and how customs and carriers use it to check the cargo.',
  'The packing list says what is in each box. Customs and the carrier use it to match the cargo to the invoice.',
  [('What it shows', ['Package numbers, type (carton, pallet, drum) and marks.', 'Contents of each package with quantity.', 'Net and gross weight, and dimensions.', 'Totals that agree with the invoice and the bill of lading.']),
   ('Why it matters', 'In an examination, the officer opens a few packages and compares. Correct counts and weights save time. Wrong weights hit the VGM and the freight bill.'),
   ('Tips', ['Number every package and mark it the same way on the pack.', 'Give the dimensions in the same unit across the sheet.', 'Include the pallet weight and the packing weight.']),
   ('Link to freight', 'Freight is charged on actual or volume weight, so accurate dimensions save you money and avoid re-weighing.')],
  [('Can it be combined with the invoice?', 'Better to keep them separate. Customs wants both.'),
   ('Is there an official format?', 'No, but it must have the details above.'),
   ('Who signs?', 'The shipper or exporter.')],
  ('document-generator', 'Open the document generator'))
g('proforma-invoice-vs-commercial-invoice', 'docs', 'Proforma invoice vs commercial invoice|difference',
  'The difference between a proforma invoice and a commercial invoice, and which one is used for payment, customs and banks.',
  'They look the same, but one is an offer and the other is the final bill.',
  [('Proforma invoice', 'A quote that states what the seller will charge. The buyer uses it to arrange payment, a licence or a letter of credit. It is not a demand for payment and not a customs document.'),
   ('Commercial invoice', 'Issued after the sale, with the real quantity, price and shipment details. Customs, the bank and the buyer use it.'),
   ('Where each is used', [('table', ['Use', 'Document'], [['Opening a letter of credit', 'Proforma'], ['Advance payment request', 'Proforma'], ['Customs clearance', 'Commercial'], ['Bank collection and final payment', 'Commercial']])]),
   ('Tip', 'Make sure the final invoice matches the proforma. A change in price or terms should be agreed in writing.')],
  [('Can I use the proforma for customs?', 'No. Customs needs the final commercial invoice.'),
   ('Does a proforma have a number?', 'Yes, but it is a separate series.'),
   ('Is it binding?', 'Only if the buyer accepts it as the contract.')],
  ('document-generator', 'Open the document generator'))
g('letter-of-credit-basics', 'docs', 'Letter of credit|how it works for exporters and importers',
  'How a letter of credit works, the parties, common types and the document rules that cause payment to be refused.',
  'A letter of credit (LC) is a bank promise to pay the seller if the seller presents the documents that the LC asks for, exactly as it asks.',
  [('Parties', ['Applicant: the buyer, who asks the bank to open it.', 'Issuing bank: the buyer bank, which makes the promise.', 'Beneficiary: the seller, who gets paid.', 'Advising or confirming bank: the seller bank, which may add its own promise.']),
   ('How it flows', 'The buyer and seller agree on an LC. The issuing bank opens it. The seller ships and presents documents to the bank. If the documents comply, the bank pays or accepts a draft.'),
   ('Why payments fail', ['Any difference between the LC terms and the documents, even a small one, can be a discrepancy.', 'Late shipment or late presentation.', 'Wrong names, amounts or descriptions.']),
   ('Good habits', 'Read the LC when it arrives, not at shipping time. Ask for amendments at once. Check your documents against the LC before you submit.')],
  [('Is an LC safe for the seller?', 'It is safer than open credit, but only if your documents comply and the issuing bank is sound.'),
   ('What is a confirmed LC?', 'A second bank adds its promise to pay, which covers the issuing bank and country risk.'),
   ('What rules apply?', 'Most LCs follow the ICC rules known as UCP 600.')],
  ('document-checker', 'Open the document checker'))
g('shipping-instructions-and-cut-off', 'docs', 'Shipping instructions (SI)|what you send the carrier',
  'What shipping instructions are, what details the carrier needs, SI cut-off and how mistakes lead to amendments and fees.',
  'Shipping instructions tell the carrier or forwarder how to prepare the bill of lading. The data you give becomes the BL.',
  [('What to send', ['Shipper, consignee and notify party with full addresses and contacts.', 'Description of goods, HS code, number of packages and weights.', 'Container number and seal when known, and the VGM.', 'Freight terms, prepaid or collect, and the number of original BLs wanted.']),
   ('SI cut-off', 'Carriers set a deadline before the vessel sails for you to submit the instructions. Miss it and the BL can be delayed, or the cargo can be rolled.'),
   ('Amendments', 'Changes after the BL is issued cost a fee, and after sailing the fees go up. Check the draft BL carefully.'),
   ('Why it matters for payment', 'A bank will refuse a BL whose details differ from the LC. Take the LC wording into the SI.')],
  [('How many copies of the BL?', 'Normally three originals, with copies. State the number you need.'),
   ('Can I send the SI by email?', 'Yes, or through the carrier portal or your forwarder.'),
   ('Who confirms the draft BL?', 'You do. Approve it only after checking it against the invoice and LC.')],
  ('bl-awb-draft', 'Open the BL and AWB draft tool'))
g('delivery-order-explained', 'docs', 'Delivery order (DO)|what it is and how to get it',
  'What a delivery order is in Indian imports, who issues it, what you need to get it and the charges that are paid first.',
  'The delivery order (DO) is the shipping line or agent authority to release the cargo to the importer. No DO, no pick-up.',
  [('Who issues it', 'The shipping line or its Indian agent, or the NVOCC or consolidator in the case of LCL cargo.'),
   ('What you need', ['The original bill of lading (or a telex release, or the surrender proof).', 'KYC and a stamped authority letter or the importer ID.', 'Payment of the local charges and any freight due.', 'The customs out of charge, for the terminal release.']),
   ('Charges you may pay at this stage', 'Destination terminal handling, documentation, DO fee, container deposit and detention or demurrage if time has run out.'),
   ('Tip', 'Start the DO process before the ship arrives. Use a destination agent who can handle the DO and the release in one step.')],
  [('What is DO validity?', 'The DO is valid for a date. If the cargo is not collected, you need an extension.'),
   ('Can a broker take the DO for me?', 'Yes, with your authority letter.'),
   ('What if the BL is lost?', 'You need an indemnity and an alternative approval from the carrier, which takes time.')],
  ('destination-do-agency', 'See our DO agency service'))
g('import-export-manifest-igm-egm', 'docs', 'IGM and EGM|the import and export manifests',
  'What the Import General Manifest (IGM) and Export General Manifest (EGM) are, who files them and why they matter for clearance and benefits.',
  'The manifest is the carrier declaration of what is on board. Customs links your bill of entry or shipping bill to it.',
  [('IGM, for imports', 'The shipping line or airline files it before arrival. It lists every consignment with the BL number, consignee, description and weight. A bill of entry cannot be completed without the IGM line.'),
   ('EGM, for exports', 'The carrier files the EGM after loading. It confirms that the goods left. Your shipping bill is closed against it, and drawback or GST refund usually follows.'),
   ('Where problems start', ['A wrong BL number or a missing house BL in the manifest.', 'A mismatch of container or weight between the manifest and your documents.', 'An EGM not filed or not matched, which blocks refunds.']),
   ('What to do', 'Check with your forwarder that the manifest is filed and matches your documents. Ask for amendments early.')],
  [('Who amends the manifest?', 'The carrier or its agent, with customs permission.'),
   ('How do I know the EGM is filed?', 'The shipping bill status in ICEGATE shows it. Our ICEGATE guide explains the status.'),
   ('Does the EGM affect GST refund?', 'Yes. The refund process depends on the EGM matching the shipping bill.')],
  ('icegate-status-guide', 'Open the ICEGATE status guide'))
g('clean-vs-claused-bill-of-lading', 'docs', 'Clean vs claused bill of lading|and why banks care',
  'What a clean and a claused bill of lading is, what remarks matter, and how to avoid a claused BL when you ship.',
  'A clean BL says the cargo was received in good order. A claused BL adds remarks about damage or poor packing, which a bank may refuse.',
  [('Clean bill of lading', 'No remarks about the condition of the goods or the packing. It is what most letters of credit require.'),
   ('Claused bill of lading', 'The carrier writes a remark, for example "cartons wet" or "rust on drums". The remark qualifies the receipt and is a red flag for the buyer and the bank.'),
   ('How to avoid it', ['Pack for the voyage and the weather.', 'Inspect the cargo and the container at stuffing.', 'Take photos and keep the stuffing report.']),
   ('If it happens', 'Ask the carrier to remove the remark, with proof. Some exporters give a letter of indemnity, but that has its own legal risk, so be careful.')],
  [('Who decides the remark?', 'The carrier or its agent at receipt of the cargo.'),
   ('Can a claused BL be paid under LC?', 'Usually not, unless the LC allows it.'),
   ('Does it affect insurance?', 'Yes. An insurer may reject claims for known damage before loading.')],
  ('bl-awb-draft', 'Open the BL and AWB draft tool'))
